Now mnt.cc: add whitelist enforcement. Add a function in mnt namespace, exposed in mnt.h so it can be unit-checked? Keep static in mnt.cc, called from initCloneNs. But for testability via shell integration tests, static is fine.

Implementation:

```cpp
static bool pathUnderWhitelist(const std::string& dst, const std::string& allowed) { ... }
```

Normalize: use std::filesystem::path lexically_normal to resolve "..". Reject non-absolute dst after normalization? Mount dsts should be absolute; the code elsewhere uses stripLeadingSlashes. A dst like "/../etc" lexically_normal → "/etc" — hmm, that would normalize to /etc and then be checked against whitelist, which is correct behavior (no escape). A dst of "../etc" normalizes to "../etc" (relative, still has ..) → reject.

Logic:

```cpp
static bool mountDstAllowed(const std::string& dst, const std::vector<std::string>& whitelist) {
    std::error_code ec;
    auto norm = fs::path(dst).lexically_normal();
    if (!norm.is_absolute()) return false;
    for (const auto& entry : whitelist) {
        auto allowed = fs::path(entry).lexically_normal();
        if (!allowed.is_absolute()) continue; // invalid whitelist entries ignored? better: validated earlier
        if (norm == allowed) return true;
        // prefix match on path components
        auto it = std::mismatch(allowed.begin(), allowed.end(), norm.begin()).first;
        if (it == allowed.end()) return true; // norm is under allowed
    }
    return false;
}
```

Wait std::mismatch with component iterators: if allowed is a prefix of norm component-wise, then norm is under allowed. E.g. allowed=/app, norm=/app/data → components [/, app] vs [/, app, data] → mismatch ends when allowed exhausted → allowed. allowed=/app, norm=/application → components [/,app] vs [/,application] → mismatch at second component → not allowed. Good, component-wise avoids the "/app" vs "/application" string-prefix bug.

Enforcement function:

```cpp
static bool enforceMountWhitelist(nsj_t* nsj) {
    if (nsj->njc.mount_whitelist_size() == 0) return true;
    // validate whitelist entries themselves
    for (const auto& entry : nsj->njc.mount_whitelist()) {
        auto allowed = fs::path(entry).lexically_normal();
        if (!allowed.is_absolute()) { LOG_E(...); return false; }
    }
    for (const auto& mpt : nsj->njc.mount()) {
        if (!mountDstAllowed(mpt.dst(), whitelist)) {
            LOG_E("Mount destination '%s' is not under any mount_whitelist entry", QC(mpt.dst()));
            return false;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

Call it in initCloneNs before building, and also in initNoCloneNs? No mounts there. Call in initCloneNs start. Note: also should apply when clone_newns is false but chroot used? Whitelist is about mounts; skip.

Note fs = std::filesystem alias is declared mid-file (line ~138) before tryCreateDir. enforceMountWhitelist placed after that alias. initCloneNs is after. Good.

mnt.h — check what's declared there. Let me view mnt.h quickly when editing. Also should the whitelist be enforced for symlink mounts (is_symlink)? Those are in njc.mount() too with dst — yes, they're MountPt entries, covered.

