Logic:

```cpp
static bool mountDstAllowed(const std::string& dst, const std::vector<std::string>& whitelist) {
    std::error_code ec;
    auto norm = fs::path(dst).lexically_normal();
    if (!norm.is_absolute()) return false;
    for (const auto& entry : whitelist) {
        auto allowed = fs::path(entry).lexically_normal();
        if (!allowed.is_absolute()) continue; // invalid whitelist entries ignored? better: validated earlier
        if (norm == allowed) return true;
        // prefix match on path components
        auto it = std::mismatch(allowed.begin(), allowed.end(), norm.begin()).first;
        if (it == allowed.end()) return true; // norm is under allowed
    }
    return false;
}
```

Wait std::mismatch with component iterators: if allowed is a prefix of norm component-wise, then norm is under allowed. E.g. allowed=/app, norm=/app/data → components [/, app] vs [/, app, data] → mismatch ends when allowed exhausted → allowed. allowed=/app, norm=/application → components [/,app] vs [/,application] → mismatch at second component → not allowed. Good, component-wise avoids the "/app" vs "/application" string-prefix bug.

